{"name":"Into the Stratosphere","hex":"#425267","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"into the stratosphere","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Prepositional phrase","definitions":["Alternative form of in the stratosphere."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/into_the_stratosphere"}],"entries":[{"title":"Stratosphere","blocks":[{"text":"The stratosphere is the second-lowest layer of Earth's atmosphere, located above the troposphere and below the mesosphere. Pronounced, the name originates from Ancient Greek  στρωτός (strōtós) 'layer, stratum' and  -sphere. The stratosphere is composed of stratified temperature zones, with the warmer layers of air located higher and the cooler layers lower. The increase of temperature with altitude is a result of the absorption of the Sun's ultraviolet (UV) radiation by the ozone layer, where ozone is exothermically photolyzed into oxygen in a cyclical fashion. This temperature inversion is in contrast to the troposphere, where temperature decreases with altitude, and between the troposphere and stratosphere is the tropopause border that demarcates the beginning of the temperature inversion."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/ISS-46_Soyuz_TMA-17M_reentry.jpg/330px-ISS-46_Soyuz_TMA-17M_reentry.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stratosphere","wikidata":"Q108376"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Layersofozone_1.jpeg_1240x510_q85_subsampling-2.jpg/500px-Layersofozone_1.jpeg_1240x510_q85_subsampling-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Layersofozone_1.jpeg_1240x510_q85_subsampling-2.jpg/500px-Layersofozone_1.jpeg_1240x510_q85_subsampling-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The ozone layer in the stratosphere blocks harmful UV radiation from reaching the surface of the Earth. A gamma-ray burst would deplete the ozone layer, allowing UV radiation through.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALayersofozone_1.jpeg_1240x510_q85_subsampling-2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Boeing_737_view_1.jpg/500px-Boeing_737_view_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Boeing_737_view_1.jpg/1280px-Boeing_737_view_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Boeing_737_view_1.jpg/500px-Boeing_737_view_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Aircraft typically cruise at the stratosphere to avoid turbulence rampant in the troposphere. The blue beam in this image is the ozone layer, beaming further to the mesosphere. The ozone heats the stratosphere, making conditions stable. The stratosphere is also the altitude limit of jets and weather balloons, as air is roughly a thousand times thinner there than at the troposphere.[11]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABoeing_737_view_1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Gigantic_jet_NOIRLab.jpg/500px-Gigantic_jet_NOIRLab.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Gigantic_jet_NOIRLab.jpg/1280px-Gigantic_jet_NOIRLab.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Gigantic_jet_NOIRLab.jpg/500px-Gigantic_jet_NOIRLab.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Lightning extending above the troposphere into the stratosphere as blue jet and reaching into the mesosphere as red sprite","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGigantic_jet_NOIRLab.jpg"}],"sounds":{},"pictures":[{"title":"The Indian grey mongoose (Urva edwardsii) mating 2","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/The_Indian_grey_mongoose_%28Urva_edwardsii%29_mating_2.jpg/500px-The_Indian_grey_mongoose_%28Urva_edwardsii%29_mating_2.jpg","ratio":1.5,"link":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Indian_grey_mongoose_(Urva_edwardsii)_mating_2.jpg","share":0.20375},{"title":"The Indian grey mongoose (Urva edwardsii) mating 1","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/The_Indian_grey_mongoose_%28Urva_edwardsii%29_mating_1.jpg/500px-The_Indian_grey_mongoose_%28Urva_edwardsii%29_mating_1.jpg","ratio":1.5,"link":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Indian_grey_mongoose_(Urva_edwardsii)_mating_1.jpg","share":0.189375},{"title":"The Indian grey mongoose (Urva edwardsii) mating 3","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/The_Indian_grey_mongoose_%28Urva_edwardsii%29_mating_3.jpg/500px-The_Indian_grey_mongoose_%28Urva_edwardsii%29_mating_3.jpg","ratio":1.5,"link":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Indian_grey_mongoose_(Urva_edwardsii)_mating_3.jpg","share":0.18125},{"title":"Stratosphere Tower, Straford, October 2025 (1)","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Stratosphere_Tower%2C_Straford%2C_October_2025_%281%29.jpg/500px-Stratosphere_Tower%2C_Straford%2C_October_2025_%281%29.jpg","ratio":0.75,"link":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stratosphere_Tower,_Straford,_October_2025_(1).jpg","share":0.161875},{"title":"The Indian grey mongoose (Urva edwardsii) mating","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/The_Indian_grey_mongoose_%28Urva_edwardsii%29_mating.jpg/500px-The_Indian_grey_mongoose_%28Urva_edwardsii%29_mating.jpg","ratio":1.5,"link":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Indian_grey_mongoose_(Urva_edwardsii)_mating.jpg","share":0.13625},{"title"